Chapter: Render
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Pond
  cites Safari chapter Tower
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Safari chapter SafariCritter

 The frame mapping: which segments are in view, and how a point authored in any of
 their frames reaches the rider's.

 A partial port of wasm/render.zig -- the spine, not yet the frame. render.zig is
 the orchestrator: one 431-line `frame` that walks the chain, lays the road strip
 and each corner's pavement, collects the trees, towers, critters, cats, rails and
 the truck, depth-sorts the lot and draws it back to front. Every one of those
 steps stands on `at (d, a, x)` -- the composition ported here -- so this is the
 piece the rest of the module has to be built on.

 WHAT IS PORTED HERE IS SHAPED BY WHAT RENDER.ZIG LETS ANYTHING SEE, and that is a
 finding rather than a preference. Its entire public surface is four names --
 Chain, Pose, at, and frame. buildChain, Mapper, mapPt, and all four ground and
 rail emitters are private to the file. So `at` is the ONE piece of this chapter
 with a direct oracle: a probe can call it and compare. The chain and the prev
 mapper are graded against what the world and geom already expose, and RenderCheck
 says exactly where that is weaker and why. PORTING_NOTES E1 records it as the
 module's shape, not a gap in the check.

 We say:

Section: The Chain

 How many segments ahead are drawn, and the fixed buffer that bounds them. The zig
 carries both because its Chain is a fixed array that the look-ahead has to fit
 inside; the port keeps both because the SMALLER of the two is what actually stops
 the walk, and which one that is should stay visible rather than being folded into
 one number.

  look-ahead : Integer
  look-ahead = 7

  max-chain : Integer
  max-chain = 8

 THE CHAIN IS A LIST OF SEGMENT INDICES. The zig's Chain is an array plus a length
 because it has to be; a Codex list carries its own length, so the record collapses
 to the list and every `ch.len` becomes `list-length ch`.

 Follow exit-to from `start`, stopping at the cap or at the terminus -- no segment
 leads out of the finish line, so the chain ends there rather than looping. `n` is
 how many indices are already down, which is the zig's `ch.len` read at the TOP of
 its loop: the cap is tested before the index is appended, so a chain is never
 longer than the cap and the terminus is never dropped for being one past it.

  build-chain : List Segment, Integer -> List Integer
  build-chain (segs) (start) = chain-from segs start 0

  chain-from : List Segment, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  chain-from (segs) (s) (n) =
    if n >= look-ahead then []
    else if n >= max-chain then []
    else if (list-at segs s).terminates then [s]
    else [s] & chain-from segs (list-at segs s).exit-to (n + 1)

Section: The Rider Pose

 Where the rider sits on his CURRENT segment and which way he is looking. `hw` is
 that segment's half width, and it is here rather than being looked up because his
 `across` is centre-relative while to-rider wants from-the-left -- the same shift
 Camera's port already threads.

 The `yaw` handed in is not his heading alone. render.zig folds the distracted
 gaze and the lean's head turn into it before building the pose, so the whole view
 swings together with the backdrop; the port takes the sum, as the zig does.

  Pose = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    hw : Real
  }

Section: The Frame Composition

 at maps (a, x) in chain[d]'s bottom-left frame into the rider's, composing the
 joins down to chain[0] and then applying the rider transform. For d = 0 there is
 nothing to compose and it is just to-rider.

 THE JOINS COMPOSE BACKWARDS, from d down to 1, and the segment that supplies each
 join is the one BELOW it. chain[k] leads to chain[k+1] through chain[k]'s OWN exit
 turn, so stepping a point from chain[k+1]'s frame down into chain[k]'s reads
 chain[k]'s length, angle, direction and width -- never chain[k+1]'s. Off by one
 here and a distant segment lands rotated by its neighbour's turn, which does not
 look like corruption; it looks like a plausible road bending the wrong way.

  compose-down : List Segment, List Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> AX
  compose-down (segs) (ch) (k) (a) (x) =
    if k <= 0 then AX { a = a, x = x }
    else let seg = list-at segs (list-at ch (k - 1))
    in let p = next-to-cur a x seg.length seg.exit-angle seg.exit-right seg.width
    in compose-down segs ch (k - 1) p.a p.x

  at : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  at (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (a) (x) =
    let p = compose-down segs ch d a x
    in to-rider p.a p.x pose.along pose.across pose.yaw pose.hw

Section: Mappers

 How to express a point authored in SOME segment's frame in the rider's. A forward
 joint maps both of its segments through the chain -- `at` at index d, above.

 THE JOINT JUST BEHIND THE RIDER IS THE ONE THAT CANNOT, and it is the reason this
 abstraction exists at all. Its `from` is the PREVIOUS segment, which is not on the
 chain: the chain starts at the rider's current segment and only ever runs forward.
 So a point authored there steps FORWARD through the join with cur-to-next -- the
 inverse of what every other point does -- and then goes straight to the rider.
 Without that pass the pavement the rider is still crossing vanishes the instant
 his segment index flips, which is the bug the behind-joint pass was written for
 and which the tower and the corner creatures each had in turn.

 The zig's Mapper is a struct with a two-valued tag; the port keeps the struct and
 narrows the tag to a Boolean, because a Codex variant carries no payload and the
 four prev fields have to live somewhere. `is-chain` reads as the zig's
 `kind == .chain`.

  Mapper = record {
    is-chain : Boolean,
    d : Integer,
    prev-len : Real,
    prev-angle : Real,
    prev-right : Boolean,
    prev-w : Real
  }

 The zig defaults every Mapper field and fills in the one or two that matter. Codex
 records are built whole, so the two constructors below take that place -- and they
 also make the two kinds impossible to half-build, which the defaults did not.

  chain-map : Integer -> Mapper
  chain-map (d) =
    Mapper { is-chain = True, d = d, prev-len = 0.0, prev-angle = 0.0, prev-right = False, prev-w = 0.0 }

  prev-map : Segment -> Mapper
  prev-map (s) =
    Mapper { is-chain = False, d = 0, prev-len = s.length, prev-angle = s.exit-angle, prev-right = s.exit-right, prev-w = s.width }

  map-pt : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real -> RiderPt
  map-pt (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (a) (x) =
    if m.is-chain
      then at segs ch pose m.d a x
      else let p = cur-to-next a x m.prev-len m.prev-angle m.prev-right m.prev-w
        in to-rider p.a p.x pose.along pose.across pose.yaw pose.hw

Section: Collection Limits and Culls

 Beyond this depth a tree draws as flat triangles rather than cone hulls, and the
 crown's foliage gradient fades to solid over this much depth. Both are drawing
 decisions, carried here because the collector is what measures the depth.

  detail-dist : Real
  detail-dist = 200.0

  crown-shade-dist : Real
  crown-shade-dist = 80.0

 Scenery shorter than this on screen is skipped.

  min-scenery-px : Real
  min-scenery-px = 2.0

  min-critter-px : Real
  min-critter-px = 2.0

 THE TWO CULLS ARE DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT AND BOTH MUST STAY LIVE. The SEGMENT
 cull saves projecting a critter at all: trees hide the farm animals past a few
 segments, and the corner safari animals carry a little further before they are
 worth it. The SIZE cull then trims whatever is left down to a genuine speck. The
 zig counts each separately every frame so a gate can assert both still fire -- a
 reach so loose the size cull never fires, or so tight the segments do all the
 work, is a bug in the budget. Those two counters are `pub`, and they are this
 chapter's only observable output.

  farm-seg-reach : Integer
  farm-seg-reach = 3

  safari-seg-reach : Integer
  safari-seg-reach = 5

 THE SIZE CULL MEASURES AGAINST THE STABLE BASE FOCAL, NOT THE LIVE ONE, and that
 is not an oversight to tidy up. The focal pulls in to 0.35x during a lean, so
 culling a critter on the live focal would yank close animals out of view the
 instant the rider turns and pop them back when he straightened. Scenery -- trees
 and the cat -- does cull on the live `cam-focal`. The two are different on
 purpose and the port keeps them different.

 The fixed-array caps. Codex lists carry their own length, so these bound nothing
 here; they are ported because they are observable IF reached, and the truncation
 below is where that would show. On this route they are not reached -- render's
 own note puts the tree peak at about 37 percent of its cap -- so the port
 truncates at the end where the zig stops mid-segment. Those differ only once a
 cap is hit, and making them agree there would mean threading a running count
 through every collector for a case this route cannot produce.

  max-vis-trees : Integer
  max-vis-trees = 640

  max-vis-towers : Integer
  max-vis-towers = 16

  max-vis-critters : Integer
  max-vis-critters = 320

  max-vis-cats : Integer
  max-vis-cats = 8

Section: Tower Placement

 Out past the corner, off to the right, and yawed. A long segment also stands its
 own tower halfway down, that far LEFT of the centreline.

  tower-beyond : Real
  tower-beyond = 160.0

  tower-right : Real
  tower-right = 20.0

  seg-tower-left : Real
  seg-tower-left = 100.0

 THE ZIG WRITES ITS OWN PI HERE, TRUNCATED, and the port copies the literal rather
 than reaching for Trig's `deg`. render.zig says `30.0 * 3.14159265 / 180.0`, which
 is 0.523598775 where thirty times a full-precision degree is 0.5235987756. The
 difference is below f32 and would almost certainly vanish at the seam -- but a
 port that silently improves a constant is a port whose disagreements no longer
 mean what they say, so the truncation is kept.

  tower-yaw : Real
  tower-yaw = 30.0 * 3.14159265 / 180.0

Section: The Collected Kinds

 The zig collects into PARALLEL ARRAYS -- t-right, t-fwd, t-h, t-col -- because it
 has to; the port collects records, which is the same data with the index made
 implicit. What the port does NOT change is that each kind is collected into its
 own list and merged only at the sort, because that is what makes the sort one
 pass over everything rather than a sort per kind.

  TreeItem = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    color : Integer
  }

 A tower is collected as the RECIPE for its base rather than as points: the mapper
 that owns its frame, its base centre and yaw, and the beacon's blink offset. It
 has to be, because the four base corners are re-derived at draw time from the
 yaw. `fwd` is the depth of the centre, which is what it sorts by.

  TowerItem = record {
    map : Mapper,
    a0 : Real,
    x0 : Real,
    yaw : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    off : Real
  }

 Every emoji animal -- cows, bulls, the pig herd, the corner safari pair, the pond
 ducks -- collects to the same billboard, which is why one list holds all of them.

  Billboard = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    cp : Integer,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

 A placement carries its own verdict rather than being dropped, so the two culls
 can be counted separately. `kept` says it survived both; `size-culled` says which
 one rejected it. The near-plane cull is SILENT in the zig -- it `continue`s
 without touching a counter -- so a placement can be neither kept nor size-culled,
 and that third state is the one the port has to preserve.

  Placed = record {
    b : Billboard,
    kept : Boolean,
    size-culled : Boolean
  }

Section: The Crossing Cat

 A cat is a billboard like the animals, but its across is not authored -- it comes
 out of the crossing clock, keyed on the rider's ROAD-ALONG GAP to it. So it needs
 something none of the other kinds do: how far down the route the chain segment it
 stands on begins.

 That gap starts NEGATIVE. The rider is already `along` into his own segment, so
 the start of chain[0] is behind him, and each further segment adds its length.
 The zig accumulates it as it walks; the port sums it, which is the same number
 and does not need the walk to carry state.

  chain-gap : List Segment, List Integer, Real, Integer -> Real
  chain-gap (w) (ch) (along) (d) =
    if d <= 0 then 0.0 - along
    else chain-gap w ch along (d - 1) + (list-at w (list-at ch (d - 1))).length

  CatItem = record {
    right : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    height : Real,
    pose-idx : Integer,
    lift : Real
  }

 THE CAT CULLS ON THE LIVE FOCAL, like the trees and unlike the critters, and it
 culls only at the near plane besides. render.zig argues the point: it is a flat
 billboard at one depth, so as the rider passes, the whole still scales and slides
 off to the left together -- it never streaks across the viewport the way a tower
 can -- and culling it earlier would vanish the collapsed cat exactly as he
 reaches it, instead of letting him pass it.

  cat-item : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Segment, Real -> List CatItem
  cat-item (w) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (gap) (sg) (v) =
    let st = cat-state sg.cat (gap + sg.cat.along) v
    in let rp = at w ch pose d sg.cat.along (st.across + sg.width / 2.0)
    in if rp.forward <= near then []
      else if sg.cat.height / rp.forward * cf < min-scenery-px then []
      else [CatItem { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = sg.cat.height, pose-idx = st.pose-idx, lift = st.lift }]

  seg-cat : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real -> List CatItem
  seg-cat (w) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (along) (v) =
    let sg = list-at w (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.has-cat then cat-item w ch pose d cf (chain-gap w ch along d) sg v else []

  walk-cats : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List CatItem
  walk-cats (w) (ch) (pose) (cf) (along) (v) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-cat w ch pose d cf along v) & walk-cats w ch pose cf along v (d + 1)

Section: Placing Billboards

 One rule for every emoji animal: map it into the rider frame, drop it if it is
 behind the near plane, size-cull it if it would project shorter than the floor.

  no-billboard : Billboard
  no-billboard = Billboard { right = 0.0, fwd = 0.0, height = 0.0, cp = 0, face-right = False }

  verdict : RiderPt, Real, Integer, Boolean -> Placed
  verdict (rp) (h) (cp) (fr) =
    if rp.forward <= near
      then Placed { b = no-billboard, kept = False, size-culled = False }
      else if h / rp.forward * focal < min-critter-px
        then Placed { b = no-billboard, kept = False, size-culled = True }
        else Placed { b = Billboard { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = h, cp = cp, face-right = fr }, kept = True, size-culled = False }

  place-critter : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Critter -> Placed
  place-critter (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) (cr) =
    let rp = at segs ch pose d cr.along (cr.across + hw)
    in verdict rp cr.height cr.codepoint cr.face-right

 The behind joint's critters map FORWARD through the join instead, so they take
 the mapper rather than a chain index. Same verdict.

  place-critter-via : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Critter -> Placed
  place-critter-via (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (hw) (cr) =
    let rp = map-pt segs ch pose m cr.along (cr.across + hw)
    in verdict rp cr.height cr.codepoint cr.face-right

 A DUCK IS AUTHORED IN THE CORNER FRAME AND MAPS RAW -- no half-width shift. Its
 `cu` is the bottom-left across directly, which is why the pond's negative cu sits
 LEFT of the left edge rather than left of the centre line. Getting this wrong
 would slide the whole pond half a lane and still look like a pond.

  place-duck : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Duck -> Placed
  place-duck (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (dk) =
    let rp = map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len + dk.p.cv) dk.p.cu
    in verdict rp duck-height duck-codepoint dk.face-right

Section: Harvesting a Placement List

 Collect the survivors and count the size-culled, over a list of placements. Two
 walks over one list rather than one walk returning a pair, because a pair would
 need a record whose only purpose is to be taken apart again.

  kept-of : List Placed, Integer -> List Billboard
  kept-of (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else if (list-at ps i).kept then [(list-at ps i).b] & kept-of ps (i + 1)
    else kept-of ps (i + 1)

  size-culled-of : List Placed, Integer -> Integer
  size-culled-of (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else if (list-at ps i).size-culled then 1 + size-culled-of ps (i + 1)
    else size-culled-of ps (i + 1)

  place-all : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, List Critter, Integer -> List Placed
  place-all (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) (crs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length crs then []
    else [place-critter segs ch pose d hw (list-at crs i)] & place-all segs ch pose d hw crs (i + 1)

  place-all-via : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, List Critter, Integer -> List Placed
  place-all-via (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (hw) (crs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length crs then []
    else [place-critter-via segs ch pose m hw (list-at crs i)] & place-all-via segs ch pose m hw crs (i + 1)

  place-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Integer -> List Placed
  place-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ducks then []
    else [place-duck segs ch pose m from-len (list-at ducks i)] & place-ducks segs ch pose m from-len (i + 1)

Section: Collecting Trees

 Trees are authored centre-relative, so the half width shifts them to
 from-the-left. They cull on the LIVE focal, unlike the critters above.

  place-tree : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Tree -> List TreeItem
  place-tree (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (hw) (tr) =
    let rp = at segs ch pose d tr.along (tr.across + hw)
    in if rp.forward <= near then []
      else if tr.height / rp.forward * cf < min-scenery-px then []
      else [TreeItem { right = rp.right, fwd = rp.forward, height = tr.height, color = tr.color }]

  seg-trees : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, List Tree, Integer -> List TreeItem
  seg-trees (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (hw) (trs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length trs then []
    else (place-tree segs ch pose d cf hw (list-at trs i)) & seg-trees segs ch pose d cf hw trs (i + 1)

Section: Collecting Towers

 A tower is collected only if its base CENTRE is in front of the near plane, and
 the centre is what it sorts by. The exit tower is keyed off its own segment; the
 mid-tower adds sixty so a long segment's two towers do not blink in step.

  tower-if-ahead : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List TowerItem
  tower-if-ahead (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (a0) (x0) (yw) (key) =
    let c = map-pt segs ch pose m a0 x0
    in if c.forward <= near then []
      else [TowerItem { map = m, a0 = a0, x0 = x0, yaw = yw, fwd = c.forward, off = beacon-offset-for key }]

  seg-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  seg-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in (tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (chain-map d) (sg.length + tower-beyond) (sg.width / 2.0 + tower-right) tower-yaw (list-at ch d))
       & (seg-mid-tower segs ch pose d)

  seg-mid-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  seg-mid-tower (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.has-mid-tower
      then tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (chain-map d) (sg.length / 2.0) (sg.width / 2.0 - seg-tower-left) 0.0 (list-at ch d + 60)
      else []

Section: Collecting Critters

  is-pond : Creature -> Boolean
  is-pond (c) =
   when c
    is DuckPond -> True
    is NoCreature -> False
    is Elephant -> False
    is Giraffe -> False
    is Zebra -> False
    is Rhino -> False

 The farm animals of one chain segment, if it is near enough to be worth
 projecting at all. Cows first, then the pig herd -- the order the zig fills its
 array in, which is the order ties break in at the sort.

  seg-farm : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real -> List Placed
  seg-farm (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= farm-seg-reach then []
      else (place-all segs ch pose d hw sg.cows 0) & (place-all segs ch pose d hw sg.pigs 0)

 The SEGMENT cull's tally: every farm animal on a chain segment beyond the reach,
 counted without being placed. This is the counter that makes the chain's own
 extent observable from outside render.zig, because it sums over exactly the chain
 indices at or past the reach.

  seg-cull-count : List Segment, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  seg-cull-count (segs) (ch) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= farm-seg-reach then list-length sg.cows + list-length sg.pigs else 0

 The corner pair at this segment's exit. Skipped at the terminus, which has no
 exit corner, and beyond the safari reach.

  seg-safari : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real -> List Placed
  seg-safari (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (hw) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if sg.terminates then []
      else if d >= safari-seg-reach then []
      else place-all segs ch pose d hw (corner-critters sg.exit-creature sg.length sg.exit-right hw) 0

 The three ducks on the pond at this segment's exit. The pond's WATER and BANK are
 ground quads and belong to the paint pass, not to this one; only the billboards
 are collected for the sort.

  seg-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  seg-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if d >= safari-seg-reach then []
      else if is-pond sg.exit-creature then place-ducks segs ch pose (chain-map d) sg.length 0
      else []

 Everything one chain segment contributes to the billboard list, in the zig's own
 fill order: farm animals, then the corner pair, then the ducks.

  seg-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  seg-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    let hw = (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0
    in (seg-farm segs ch pose d hw) & (seg-safari segs ch pose d hw) & (seg-ducks segs ch pose d)

Section: Walking the Chain

  walk-trees : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> List TreeItem
  walk-trees (segs) (ch) (pose) (cf) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-trees segs ch pose d cf ((list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).trees 0)
         & walk-trees segs ch pose cf (d + 1)

  walk-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  walk-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-towers segs ch pose d) & walk-towers segs ch pose (d + 1)

  walk-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  walk-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-billboards segs ch pose d) & walk-billboards segs ch pose (d + 1)

  walk-seg-cull : List Segment, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  walk-seg-cull (segs) (ch) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then 0
    else seg-cull-count segs ch d + walk-seg-cull segs ch (d + 1)

Section: The Joint Just Behind

 The segment the rider just left owns a corner, and everything on it -- pavement,
 tower, creatures, pond -- pops out the instant his segment index flips unless it
 is drawn through the join. The route is finite, so there is a previous segment
 everywhere EXCEPT the start.

 THE BEHIND CRITTERS TAKE NO REACH TEST. The forward ones are gated on
 safari-seg-reach; this pair is one join away by construction, so the zig does not
 test it and neither does the port.

  behind-billboards : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  behind-billboards (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in (place-all-via segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (pv.width / 2.0) (corner-critters pv.exit-creature pv.length pv.exit-right (pv.width / 2.0)) 0)
       & (behind-ducks segs ch pose pv)

  behind-ducks : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Segment -> List Placed
  behind-ducks (segs) (ch) (pose) (pv) =
    if is-pond pv.exit-creature then place-ducks segs ch pose (prev-map pv) pv.length 0 else []

  behind-tower : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  behind-tower (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in tower-if-ahead segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (pv.length + tower-beyond) (pv.width / 2.0 + tower-right) tower-yaw prev-idx

Section: The Corner's Outer Shoulders

 Both the corner pavement and the guard rail hang off the same three points: each
 segment's OUTER shoulder at the join, and the apex Q where the two extended
 shoulders cross. Which edge is outer flips with the turn -- a right turn's outer
 edge is the left one.

  outer-cu : Boolean, Real -> Real
  outer-cu (exit-right) (wd) = if exit-right then 0.0 else wd

 Q is found by extending each shoulder one metre past the join and intersecting
 the two lines. A real turn has a non-zero angle, so they are never parallel.

  joint-apex : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> RiderPt
  joint-apex (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let fcu = outer-cu exit-right from-w
    in let tx = outer-cu exit-right to-w
    in line-meet (map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu) (map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len + 1.0) fcu) (map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 tx) (map-pt segs ch pose to-map 1.0 tx)

Section: The Guard Rail Path

 Append points from `from` EXCLUSIVE to `to` inclusive at about one metre spacing
 -- one rail post per metre along a straight leg of the corner. At least one step
 always, so a zero-length leg still lands its endpoint.

  leg-steps : Real -> Integer
  leg-steps (dist) =
    let r = round-real dist
    in real-to-int (if r < 1.0 then 1.0 else r)

  leg-points : RiderPt, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List RiderPt
  leg-points (from) (dr) (df) (steps) (i) =
    if i > steps then []
    else let t = real-from-int i / real-from-int steps
    in [RiderPt { right = from.right + dr * t, forward = from.forward + df * t }] & leg-points from dr df steps (i + 1)

  push-leg : RiderPt, RiderPt -> List RiderPt
  push-leg (from) (to) =
    let dr = to.right - from.right
    in let df = to.forward - from.forward
    in leg-points from dr df (leg-steps (real-sqrt (dr * dr + df * df))) 1

 The run-up counts DOWN to the end edge, so the last point of the run-up is the
 outer corner itself and the first leg starts from it.

  rail-run-up : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  rail-run-up (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (cu) (k) =
    if k < 0 then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len - real-from-int k) cu] & rail-run-up segs ch pose m from-len cu (k - 1)

  rail-run-out : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  rail-run-out (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (x) (k) =
    if k > rail-runout then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (real-from-int k) x] & rail-run-out segs ch pose m x (k + 1)

 The whole corner path: run-up along the from segment's outer shoulder, the two
 legs into and out of the apex, and the run-out along the to segment's. guard_rail
 then raises that path to a bar strip and its posts.

  joint-rail-path : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RiderPt
  joint-rail-path (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let fcu = outer-cu exit-right from-w
    in let tx = outer-cu exit-right to-w
    in let of-pt = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu
    in let ot-pt = map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 tx
    in let q = joint-apex segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right
    in (rail-run-up segs ch pose from-map from-len fcu rail-runout) & (push-leg of-pt q) & (push-leg q ot-pt) & (rail-run-out segs ch pose to-map tx 1)

  joint-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RailPoly
  joint-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    rail-emit (joint-rail-path segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right)

 Every FORWARD joint on the chain, in chain order, then the joint behind. That is
 the order the zig's rail store holds them in, and the order ties break in.

  walk-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  walk-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d + 1 >= list-length ch then []
    else (joint-rails segs ch pose (chain-map d) (chain-map (d + 1)) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width (list-at segs (list-at ch (d + 1))).width (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).exit-right)
         & walk-rails segs ch pose (d + 1)

  behind-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  behind-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in joint-rails segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (chain-map 0) pv.length pv.width (list-at segs (list-at ch 0)).width pv.exit-right

Section: The Chased Truck

 Walk the truck's LEAD -- its route distance minus the rider's -- forward along
 the chain to find which segment its centre lands on. It sorts at that centre-line
 depth, one item however long the body is.

  TruckAt = record {
    present : Boolean,
    d : Integer,
    along : Real,
    fwd : Real
  }

  no-truck : TruckAt
  no-truck = TruckAt { present = False, d = 0, along = 0.0, fwd = 0.0 }

  truck-step : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> TruckAt
  truck-step (segs) (ch) (pose) (remaining) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then no-truck
    else if remaining > (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length
      then truck-step segs ch pose (remaining - (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length) (d + 1)
      else truck-here segs ch pose remaining d

  truck-here : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Integer -> TruckAt
  truck-here (segs) (ch) (pose) (remaining) (d) =
    let c = at segs ch pose d remaining ((list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width / 2.0)
    in if c.forward > near
      then TruckAt { present = True, d = d, along = remaining, fwd = c.forward }
      else no-truck

 A lead of zero or less means the truck is level with or behind the rider, and it
 is simply not drawn -- the zig does not clamp it forward.

  truck-at : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real -> TruckAt
  truck-at (segs) (ch) (pose) (along) (lead) =
    if lead > 0.0 then truck-step segs ch pose (along + lead) 0 else no-truck

Section: The Depth Sort

 One order over everything that stands up off the ground, far to near, so a tree
 occludes a rail and a rail occludes a cow by honest depth rather than by paint
 order. This is what guard_rail's collect-then-sort design exists to feed, and why
 its RailPoly carries a `fwd` it never draws with.

  Kind =
   | KTree
   | KTower
   | KCow
   | KCat
   | KTruck
   | KRail

  Item = record {
    fwd : Real,
    kind : Kind,
    i : Integer
  }

  tree-items : List TreeItem, Integer -> List Item
  tree-items (ts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at ts i).fwd, kind = KTree, i = i }] & tree-items ts (i + 1)

  tower-items : List TowerItem, Integer -> List Item
  tower-items (ts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ts then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at ts i).fwd, kind = KTower, i = i }] & tower-items ts (i + 1)

  cow-items : List Billboard, Integer -> List Item
  cow-items (bs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at bs i).fwd, kind = KCow, i = i }] & cow-items bs (i + 1)

  cat-items : List CatItem, Integer -> List Item
  cat-items (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at cs i).fwd, kind = KCat, i = i }] & cat-items cs (i + 1)

  rail-items : List RailPoly, Integer -> List Item
  rail-items (rs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length rs then []
    else [Item { fwd = (list-at rs i).fwd, kind = KRail, i = i }] & rail-items rs (i + 1)

  truck-items : TruckAt -> List Item
  truck-items (t) = if t.present then [Item { fwd = t.fwd, kind = KTruck, i = 0 }] else []

 THE SORT MUST BE STABLE AND THE ZIG'S IS. Its insertion loop moves a key left
 only while the element before it is STRICTLY nearer-to-far -- `items[j-1].fwd <
 key.fwd` -- so equal depths keep the order they were merged in. That matters more
 here than it looks: a rail bar and its post share a corner and can land on the
 same depth, and so can two roadside trees on opposite shoulders.

 `core/Sort` EXISTS AND MUST NOT BE SUBSTITUTED HERE, which is worth saying before
 the merge is described, because the shape of this chapter invites the swap. The
 foreword's `sort-by : List a, (a, a -> Integer) -> List a` looks like exactly what
 this wants and is an in-place QUICKSORT with median-of-three pivoting. Quicksort
 swaps across equal keys; it is not stable and its chapter claims no stability.

 STABILITY IS LOAD-BEARING HERE AND THE EVIDENCE IS A BUG THAT HAPPENED. Ties are
 real -- render.zig sorts by an f32 depth and equal depths keep merge order -- and
 PORTING_NOTES D9 records a rail post and a rail bar swapping places in a
 3,091-command stream, one adjacent transposition with every other command
 identical, invisible to every per-module check because both modules were right.
 Handing this to `sort-by` would reintroduce that class silently and only the
 whole-frame check would ever say so. De-duplicating against the foreword is right
 far more often than it is wrong; this is one of the times it is wrong.

 THE PORT SORTS BY MERGING RATHER THAN BY INSERTING, and that is the one place in
 this chapter where the algorithm departs from the zig on purpose. A stable merge
 sort and a stable insertion sort produce the SAME total order -- same comparison,
 same tie rule -- so nothing observable changes. What changes is the cost, and on
 persistent lists the difference is not a constant factor.

 Insertion sort on a mutable array shifts elements in place. On a Codex list there
 is nothing to shift: rebuilding the tail past the insertion point allocates a list
 per element, so ONE insert is quadratic in allocations and the whole sort is
 cubic. Measured, it is not slow but fatal -- seven hundred items across eight
 frames exhausted codexzig's whole 4 GiB bump heap, which never reclaims (C6). The
 merge is quadratic in copies and comes in around a hundred megabytes.

 So the rule this is an instance of: PORTING AN ALGORITHM FAITHFULLY MEANS PORTING
 ITS ANSWER, NOT ITS COST MODEL. An in-place algorithm carried literally onto
 persistent lists can gain a whole complexity class, and the bump heap turns that
 into a crash rather than a wait. See PORTING_NOTES E4.

  rest-from : List Item, Integer -> List Item
  rest-from (ys) (j) =
    if j >= list-length ys then []
    else [list-at ys j] & rest-from ys (j + 1)

 Merge two runs already in far-to-near order. `a` wins ties -- the test asks
 whether b is STRICTLY deeper -- which is what makes the merge stable and matches
 the zig's strict `<`.

  merge-items : List Item, List Item, Integer, Integer -> List Item
  merge-items (a) (b) (i) (j) =
    if i >= list-length a then rest-from b j
    else if j >= list-length b then rest-from a i
    else if deeper-than (list-at b j).fwd (list-at a i).fwd
      then [list-at b j] & merge-items a b i (j + 1)
      else [list-at a i] & merge-items a b (i + 1) j

 THE SORT DECLINES TO ORDER TWO DEPTHS IT CANNOT TELL APART, and that is the one
 place this chapter's comparison is not the zig's character for character. The
 zig's key is an f32; the port's is a Real, which is f64 in every plug. Two depths
 that differ by less than the f32 grid are THE SAME NUMBER in the game -- its sort
 sees a tie and keeps merge order -- while the port can see a difference and would
 order them by it. Sorting on precision the oracle does not have is not fidelity;
 it is the port inventing an order.

 So `deeper-than` is a strict comparison with half an f32 ulp of slack, and the
 slack is measured rather than chosen. A rail post and a rail bar in the whole
 frame check land within 6e-8 of each other and swap without it; at 2e-8 they
 still swap, so the gap is between a sixth and a half of an ulp. Half an ulp is
 the largest threshold that cannot tie two depths the game holds apart, since two
 distinct f32s differ by a whole one.

 A grader that measures inversions has to use the same rule -- RenderCheck's does,
 and its own prose says why. A property asserted at a finer resolution than the
 sort works at reports the rule as a defect.

  sort-tie : Real
  sort-tie = 0.00000006

  deeper-than : Real, Real -> Boolean
  deeper-than (x) (y) = x - y > sort-tie * real-max (real-abs y) 1.0

  sort-items : List Item -> List Item
  sort-items (xs) =
    if list-length xs <= 1 then xs
    else merge-items (sort-items (list-take xs (list-length xs / 2))) (sort-items (list-drop xs (list-length xs / 2))) 0 0

Section: The Frame's Collection

 Everything the chain walk gathers, plus the two cull tallies and the one depth
 order over the lot. What this does NOT carry is the ground -- the road strips,
 the corner pavement and the pond's water and bank are painted inside the same
 walk in the zig, but they are quads on the floor and never enter the sort, so
 they belong to the paint pass and are not ported here.

 ALL SEVEN KINDS ARE COLLECTED NOW. The cat was the last, and what unblocked it
 was not writing the logarithm its `pow(b, 0.7)` seemed to need: 0.75 is dyadic
 where 0.7 is not, so the front-loaded leap is a pair of square roots and the
 approximation is bounded at 8.6 cm for two frames. Safari chapter Cat argues it.

  Collected = record {
    trees : List TreeItem,
    towers : List TowerItem,
    cows : List Billboard,
    cats : List CatItem,
    rails : List RailPoly,
    truck : TruckAt,
    order : List Item,
    cull-seg : Integer,
    cull-size : Integer
  }

  prev-index : Integer -> Integer
  prev-index (seg-idx) = if seg-idx > 0 then seg-idx - 1 else 0

  all-towers : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List TowerItem
  all-towers (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-towers segs ch pose 0) & (behind-tower segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-towers segs ch pose 0

  all-placed : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Placed
  all-placed (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-billboards segs ch pose 0) & (behind-billboards segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-billboards segs ch pose 0

  all-rails : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List RailPoly
  all-rails (segs) (ch) (pose) (seg-idx) =
    if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-rails segs ch pose 0) & (behind-rails segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1))
      else walk-rails segs ch pose 0

 The merge order is the zig's own array order -- trees, towers, billboards, the
 truck, then the rails -- and since the sort is stable it is also the order equal
 depths come out in.

  collect : List Segment, Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Real, Real -> Collected
  collect (segs) (seg-idx) (pose) (cf) (along) (v) (truck-pos) =
    let ch = build-chain segs seg-idx
    in let placed = all-placed segs ch pose seg-idx
    in let trees = list-take (walk-trees segs ch pose cf 0) max-vis-trees
    in let towers = list-take (all-towers segs ch pose seg-idx) max-vis-towers
    in let cows = list-take (kept-of placed 0) max-vis-critters
    in let cats = list-take (walk-cats segs ch pose cf along v 0) max-vis-cats
    in let rails = all-rails segs ch pose seg-idx
    in let tk = truck-at segs ch pose along (truck-pos - route-distance segs seg-idx along)
    in Collected {
      trees = trees,
      towers = towers,
      cows = cows,
      cats = cats,
      rails = rails,
      truck = tk,
      order = sort-items ((tree-items trees 0) & (tower-items towers 0) & (cow-items cows 0) & (cat-items cats 0) & (truck-items tk) & (rail-items rails 0)),
      cull-seg = walk-seg-cull segs ch 0,
      cull-size = size-culled-of placed 0
    }

Section: The Ground

 The road surface and everything paved: road strips, each corner's approach and
 pavement, and the pond's water and bank. These are quads on the FLOOR. They never
 enter the depth sort -- they are painted first, in walk order, and everything that
 stands up is sorted and painted over them.

  road-color : Integer
  road-color = 3421500

 Road strips are sliced this long so a bend reads smooth rather than as one long
 flat quad cutting the curve.

  road-chunk : Real
  road-chunk = 25.0

 Metres of approach road a joint paints behind its end edge. It is what covers the
 joint when the joint is the one BEHIND the rider.

  entry-road-dist : Real
  entry-road-dist = 40.0

 A ground vertex drops below the rider's tangent plane by the curvature at its
 horizontal distance, which is what bends the road toward a finite horizon instead
 of a vanishing point at infinity. The sign is the thing to get right: ground-drop
 returns a positive distance and the height is its NEGATIVE.

  ground-vert : RiderPt -> Vec3
  ground-vert (p) =
    Vec3 { right = p.right, forward = p.forward, height = 0.0 - ground-drop p.right p.forward }

  ground-verts : List RiderPt, Integer -> List Vec3
  ground-verts (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [ground-vert (list-at ps i)] & ground-verts ps (i + 1)

 THE EIGHT-POINT GUARD IS THE ZIG'S FIXED BUFFER AND IT IS KEPT. A caller handing
 in more than eight points gets nothing at all rather than a truncated polygon,
 which is the zig's behaviour and is the safer of the two: a silently shortened
 ground quad would be a wrong shape painted confidently.

  emit-ground-color : List RiderPt, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-ground-color (ps) (color) (cf) (view-w) =
    if list-length ps > 8 then []
    else ground-clip (clip-near (ground-verts ps 0) near) color cf view-w

  ground-clip : List Vec3, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  ground-clip (vs) (color) (cf) (view-w) =
    if list-length vs < 3 then []
    else push-poly color (project-all vs cf view-w 0)

  emit-ground : List RiderPt, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-ground (ps) (cf) (view-w) = emit-ground-color ps road-color cf view-w

Section: The Road Strip

 The zig takes the ceiling of length over the chunk size and floors it at one
 chunk. Codex's foreword has a floor and no ceiling, so this is the identity
 ceil x = -floor(-x) rather than a second rounding rule.

  ceil-real : Real -> Real
  ceil-real (x) = 0.0 - floor-real (0.0 - x)

  chunks-for : Real -> Integer
  chunks-for (len) =
    let c = ceil-real (len / road-chunk)
    in real-to-int (if c < 1.0 then 1.0 else c)

 EACH SLICE IS CUT FROM THE SEGMENT LENGTH BY INDEX, not accumulated by adding a
 chunk at a time. It matters: the zig computes a0 and a1 as length*i/n and
 length*(i+1)/n, so the last slice ends exactly on the segment end and the strip
 cannot drift short of the joint by an accumulated rounding.

  road-slice : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  road-slice (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) (ci) (n) (len) (wd) =
    if ci >= n then []
    else (emit-ground (slice-quad segs ch pose d ci n len wd) cf view-w)
         & road-slice segs ch pose d cf view-w (ci + 1) n len wd

  slice-quad : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Integer, Integer, Real, Real -> List RiderPt
  slice-quad (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ci) (n) (len) (wd) =
    let a0 = len * real-from-int ci / real-from-int n
    in let a1 = len * real-from-int (ci + 1) / real-from-int n
    in [at segs ch pose d a0 0.0, at segs ch pose d a0 wd, at segs ch pose d a1 wd, at segs ch pose d a1 0.0]

  seg-road : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-road (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in road-slice segs ch pose d cf view-w 0 (chunks-for sg.length) sg.length sg.width

Section: A Joint's Ground

 The approach road is the `from` segment's tail, its end edge drawn back
 entry-road-dist. The corner pavement is a quad from the INNER fuse-corner out to
 the outer apex Q: inner corner, `from`'s outer corner, Q, `to`'s outer corner.

 THE INNER CORNER IS THE EDGE THE TURN GOES TOWARD and the outer is the one it
 goes away from, so both flip with the turn direction and they flip oppositely.
 Getting the pair the wrong way round still paints a quad -- just one folded
 through the intersection.

  joint-approach : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real -> List RiderPt
  joint-approach (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (from-len) (from-w) =
    [map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len 0.0,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len from-w,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len - entry-road-dist) from-w,
     map-pt segs ch pose from-map (from-len - entry-road-dist) 0.0]

  joint-pavement : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean -> List RiderPt
  joint-pavement (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) =
    let inner = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len (if exit-right then from-w else 0.0)
    in let outer-from = map-pt segs ch pose from-map from-len (outer-cu exit-right from-w)
    in let outer-to = map-pt segs ch pose to-map 0.0 (outer-cu exit-right to-w)
    in let q = joint-apex segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right
    in [inner, outer-from, q, outer-to]

  emit-joint-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Mapper, Real, Real, Real, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-joint-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (from-map) (to-map) (from-len) (from-w) (to-w) (exit-right) (cf) (view-w) =
    (emit-ground (joint-approach segs ch pose from-map from-len from-w) cf view-w)
    & (emit-ground (joint-pavement segs ch pose from-map to-map from-len from-w to-w exit-right) cf view-w)

Section: The Pond's Ground

 The water then the bank, authored in the incoming corner frame: corner (cu, cv)
 is map (from-len + cv, cu). The ducks that float on it are billboards and were
 collected for the depth sort; only the two ground shapes are painted here.

  pond-shape : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, List PondPt, Integer -> List RiderPt
  pond-shape (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [map-pt segs ch pose m (from-len + (list-at ps i).cv) (list-at ps i).cu] & pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len ps (i + 1)

  emit-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Mapper, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (m) (from-len) (cf) (view-w) =
    (emit-ground-color (pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len water-outline 0) water-color cf view-w)
    & (emit-ground-color (pond-shape segs ch pose m from-len bank 0) bank-color cf view-w)

Section: Walking the Ground

 One chain segment's floor: its road strip, then its exit joint's ground when the
 next segment is in view, then the pond at that exit if there is one. The pond
 ground takes NO reach test -- the ducks on it do, but the water is painted
 wherever the corner is drawn at all.

  seg-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    (seg-road segs ch pose d cf view-w)
    & (seg-joint-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w)
    & (seg-pond-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w)

  seg-joint-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-joint-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    if d + 1 >= list-length ch then []
    else emit-joint-ground segs ch pose (chain-map d) (chain-map (d + 1)) (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).length (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).width (list-at segs (list-at ch (d + 1))).width (list-at segs (list-at ch d)).exit-right cf view-w

  seg-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  seg-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in if is-pond sg.exit-creature
      then emit-pond-ground segs ch pose (chain-map d) sg.length cf view-w
      else []

  walk-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  walk-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (cf) (view-w) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else (seg-ground segs ch pose d cf view-w) & walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w (d + 1)

  behind-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  behind-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (prev-idx) (cf) (view-w) =
    let pv = list-at segs prev-idx
    in (emit-joint-ground segs ch pose (prev-map pv) (chain-map 0) pv.length pv.width (list-at segs (list-at ch 0)).width pv.exit-right cf view-w)
       & (behind-pond-ground segs ch pose pv cf view-w)

  behind-pond-ground : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Segment, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  behind-pond-ground (segs) (ch) (pose) (pv) (cf) (view-w) =
    if is-pond pv.exit-creature
      then emit-pond-ground segs ch pose (prev-map pv) pv.length cf view-w
      else []

 The whole floor for one frame, forward chain then the joint behind.

  frame-ground : List Segment, Integer, Pose, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  frame-ground (segs) (seg-idx) (pose) (cf) (view-w) =
    let ch = build-chain segs seg-idx
    in if seg-idx > 0
      then (walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w 0) & (behind-ground segs ch pose (seg-idx - 1) cf view-w)
      else walk-ground segs ch pose cf view-w 0
